      <section data-sid="csr-problem" class="section sec-panel active">
            <p class="eyebrow">01 · The Problem</p>
            <h2 class="sec-title">Why Did Angular Need <span>SSR</span> at All?</h2>
            <p class="sec-lead">
              Angular started as a browser-first framework. In 2016 on fast desktops that was fine.
              But 3 billion people now browse on mobile — often on slow networks. Two things broke.
            </p>
            <div class="sec-stickers">
              <div class="sticker sticker-red">
                <div class="sticker-header">
                  <span class="sticker-icon">📱</span>
                  <span class="sticker-label">4–6 second blank screen</span>
                </div>
                <p class="card-body">500KB JS downloads, Angular boots, builds every DOM node — <strong style="color:var(--red);">then</strong> content appears. 53% of mobile users abandon after 3 seconds.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="sticker sticker-yellow">
                <div class="sticker-header">
                  <span class="sticker-icon">🔍</span>
                  <span class="sticker-label">Invisible to search engines</span>
                </div>
                <p class="card-body">Google's crawler sees an empty shell. LinkedIn and Twitter <strong>never run JavaScript</strong> for link previews — blank card every time you share a URL.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="sticker sticker-green">
                <div class="sticker-header">
                  <span class="sticker-icon">💡</span>
                  <span class="sticker-label">The fix: run Angular on the server</span>
                </div>
                <p class="card-body">Server builds the full HTML page, sends it ready-made. User sees content in milliseconds. Crawler sees full content.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

          <!-- Right: CSR Timeline -->
            <div class="timeline-card">
              <header class="tl-card-title">CSR — What the user actually experiences</header>
              <div class="timeline">
                {[
                  { n:'1', cls:'bad',  label:'Request',       sub:'Blank screen',     time:'0ms',    tc:'bad' },
                  { n:'2', cls:'bad',  label:'Empty HTML',    sub:'+ 500KB JS',       time:'~50ms',  tc:'bad' },
                  { n:'3', cls:'bad',  label:'JS Downloads',  sub:'Parse + execute',  time:'~400ms', tc:'bad' },
                  { n:'4', cls:'bad',  label:'Angular Boots', sub:'Modules init',     time:'~800ms', tc:'bad' },
                  { n:'5', cls:'good', label:'DOM Renders',   sub:'User sees content',time:'2–5s',   tc:'good'},
                ].map(({ n, cls, label, sub, time, tc }) => (
                  <div class={`tl-step ${cls}`}>
                    <div class={`tl-dot ${cls}`}>{n}</div>
                    <div class="tl-label">{label}</div>
                    <div class="tl-sub">{sub}</div>
                    <span class={`tl-time ${tc}`}>{time}</span>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
              <p class="tl-card-note">
                Google may give up before step 5. Your page never gets indexed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- 3 Browser Systems -->
            <div class="browser-systems">
              <header class="subsec-label">Under the hood — 3 systems inside every browser</header>
              <p class="card-body">
                To understand why CSR is slow and SSR is fast, you need to know that the browser has
                <strong style="color:var(--ink);">three separate systems</strong> — and each one plays a different role in putting pixels on screen.
              </p>
              <div class="worker-cards">
                <div class="worker-card worker-card-blue">
                  <header class="worker-tag worker-tag-blue">① HTML Parser</header>
                  <p class="worker-action">Reads HTML text → builds DOM nodes</p>
                  <p class="card-body">Runs automatically when HTML arrives. No JavaScript needed. Reads the string character by character and creates a live tree of DOM node objects in memory.</p>
                  <p class="worker-trigger worker-trigger-blue">Triggered by: server sending HTML</p>
                </div>
                <div class="worker-card worker-card-orange">
                  <header class="worker-tag worker-tag-orange">② DOM API</header>
                  <p class="worker-action">JavaScript's interface to create/modify DOM nodes &amp; attach events</p>
                  <p class="card-body"><code style="font-size:.67rem;background:var(--orange-pale);padding:.1rem .3rem;border-radius:6px;">document.createElement()</code>, <code style="font-size:.67rem;background:var(--orange-pale);padding:.1rem .3rem;border-radius:6px;">addEventListener()</code> — this is how Angular talks to the DOM.</p>
                  <p class="worker-trigger worker-trigger-orange">Triggered by: Angular calling it</p>
                </div>
                <div class="worker-card worker-card-green">
                  <header class="worker-tag worker-tag-green">③ Rendering Engine</header>
                  <p class="worker-action">Reads DOM tree → calculates layout → paints pixels</p>
                  <p class="card-body">Blink (Chrome), WebKit (Safari), Gecko (Firefox). Watches the DOM for changes and automatically repaints. No JS involved.</p>
                  <p class="worker-trigger worker-trigger-green">Triggered by: DOM tree changing</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="dom-note">
                <strong style="color:var(--ink);">The DOM tree is the shared data structure</strong> all three talk through.
                HTML Parser <em>writes</em> to it. DOM API (Angular) <em>writes</em> to it. Rendering Engine <em>reads</em> from it and paints.
                They never talk to each other directly.
              </div>

              <header class="subsec-label">How each worker does its job</header>
              <div class="worker-detail-cards">

                <div class="worker-detail-card worker-detail-blue">
                  <header class="worker-name worker-name-blue">Worker 1 — HTML Parser</header>
                  <p class="worker-body card-body">The server sends a raw text string. The HTML Parser reads it character by character. Every time it sees a complete tag, it <strong style="color:var(--ink);">creates a DOM node object in memory</strong>. No JavaScript. No painting. Text in → DOM nodes out.</p>
                  <div class="worker-detail-code code-wrap">
                    <div class="code-body code-body-sm" style="line-height:1.9;">
                      <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Server sends this raw text string over HTTP:</span><br/>
                      <span class="st">"&lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;World&lt;/p&gt;"</span><br/>
                      <br/>
                      <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// HTML Parser reads character by character:</span><br/>
                      <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">//   sees &lt;h1&gt;  → CREATE h1 node in DOM tree</span><br/>
                      <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">//   sees Hello → CREATE text node, attach to h1</span><br/>
                      <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">//   sees &lt;/h1&gt; → close h1</span><br/>
                      <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">//   sees &lt;p&gt;   → CREATE p node  ...and so on</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <div class="worker-detail-card worker-detail-orange">
                  <header class="worker-name worker-name-orange">Worker 2 — DOM API (JavaScript's writing interface)</header>
                  <p class="worker-body card-body">JavaScript cannot touch the DOM tree directly — it goes through the DOM API. <strong style="color:var(--ink);">Angular is the writer. DOM API is the door. The DOM tree is what changes.</strong></p>
                  <div class="worker-detail-code code-wrap">
                    <div class="code-body code-body-sm" style="line-height:1.9;">
                      <span class="ky">const</span> h1 = document.<span class="fn">createElement</span>(<span class="st">'h1'</span>)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// CREATE h1 node</span><br/>
                      h1.textContent = <span class="st">'Hello'</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// attach text node</span><br/>
                      document.body.<span class="fn">appendChild</span>(h1)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// INSERT into DOM tree</span><br/>
                      <br/>
                      <span class="ky">const</span> btn = document.<span class="fn">createElement</span>(<span class="st">'button'</span>)<br/>
                      btn.<span class="fn">addEventListener</span>(<span class="st">'click'</span>, fn)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// attach event to node</span><br/>
                      document.body.<span class="fn">appendChild</span>(btn)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// INSERT into DOM tree</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <div class="worker-detail-card worker-detail-green">
                  <header class="worker-name worker-name-green">Worker 3 — Rendering Engine (Blink in Chrome)</header>
                  <p class="worker-body card-body">Watches the DOM tree. The moment any node is added or changed — by either the HTML Parser or DOM API — it wakes up automatically. <strong style="color:var(--ink);">Never talks to JS. Never reads HTML strings. Only reads the DOM tree.</strong></p>
                  <div class="worker-detail-code code-wrap">
                    <div class="code-body code-body-sm" style="line-height:1.9;">
                      <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// DOM tree changed → Rendering Engine wakes up automatically:</span><br/>
                      <br/>
                      <span class="ky">1.</span> Style calculation&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">→ which CSS applies to each node</span><br/>
                      <span class="ky">2.</span> Layout&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">→ where each node sits (x, y, width, height)</span><br/>
                      <span class="ky">3.</span> Paint&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">→ draw actual pixels on screen → user sees content</span>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

              </div>

              <!-- CSR vs SSR 7-step flows -->
              <header class="subsec-label">How these 3 systems work in CSR vs SSR</header>
              <div class="flow-section">

                <!-- CSR 7 steps -->
                <div class="flow-block flow-block-csr">
                  <div class="flow-block-header">
                    <header class="flow-header">CSR — Angular builds the DOM</header>
                  </div>
                  <div class="flow-block-body">
                    {[
                      ['flow-step-red',    'flow-step-num-red',    '1','HTML Parser gets empty HTML','Builds skeleton DOM: just &lt;app-root&gt;&lt;/app-root&gt; — no content inside.'],
                      ['flow-step-red',    'flow-step-num-red',    '2','Rendering Engine paints it','Paints the empty skeleton → <strong>blank screen</strong>. User sees nothing.'],
                      ['flow-step-orange', 'flow-step-num-orange', '3','main.js downloads + executes','defer held it until DOM was ready. Now Angular\'s bootstrapApplication() runs.'],
                      ['flow-step-orange', 'flow-step-num-orange', '4','Angular creates Component Objects','new HelloComponent() — data and methods in memory. No DOM yet.'],
                      ['flow-step-orange', 'flow-step-num-orange', '5','Angular calls DOM API','createElement(\'h1\'), appendChild() — Angular itself builds every DOM node.'],
                      ['flow-step-green',  'flow-step-num-green',  '6','Rendering Engine repaints','Sees new DOM nodes → paints pixels → user finally sees content. (~2–5s total)'],
                      ['flow-step-green',  'flow-step-num-green',  '7','Angular attaches event listeners','addEventListener() on the existing DOM nodes. Page is now interactive.'],
                    ].map(([stepCls, numCls, n, label, body]) => (
                      <div class={`flow-step ${stepCls}`}>
                        <span class={`flow-step-num ${numCls}`}>{n}</span>
                        <div>
                          <p class="step-label">{label}</p>
                          <p class="step-body" set:html={body}></p>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                    ))}
                  </div>
                </div>

                <!-- SSR 7 steps -->
                <div class="flow-block flow-block-ssr">
                  <div class="flow-block-header">
                    <header class="flow-header">SSR — HTML Parser builds the DOM</header>
                  </div>
                  <div class="flow-block-body">
                    {[
                      ['flow-step-blue',  'flow-step-num-blue',  '1','Server runs Angular in Node.js','Creates Component Objects, evaluates templates, produces a full HTML string. Sends it over HTTP.'],
                      ['flow-step-green', 'flow-step-num-green', '2','HTML Parser gets full HTML','Builds complete DOM: &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt; — all content, fully populated.'],
                      ['flow-step-green', 'flow-step-num-green', '3','Rendering Engine paints it','Full DOM → full content on screen <strong>immediately</strong>. User sees page before JS even loads. ✅'],
                      ['flow-step-blue',  'flow-step-num-blue',  '4','main.js downloads + executes','Was downloading in parallel. DOM was already ready. bootstrapApplication() runs.'],
                      ['flow-step-blue',  'flow-step-num-blue',  '5','Angular creates Component Objects','Fresh new HelloComponent() in browser memory. Server objects are gone — these are new.'],
                      ['flow-step-blue',  'flow-step-num-blue',  '6','Hydration — DOM API reads, not writes','Angular does NOT call createElement(). It finds existing nodes and connects them to Component Objects.'],
                      ['flow-step-green', 'flow-step-num-green', '7','Angular attaches event listeners','Same as CSR: addEventListener() on existing nodes. Page is now interactive. ✅'],
                    ].map(([stepCls, numCls, n, label, body]) => (
                      <div class={`flow-step ${stepCls}`}>
                        <span class={`flow-step-num ${numCls}`}>{n}</span>
                        <div>
                          <p class="step-label">{label}</p>
                          <p class="step-body" set:html={body}></p>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                    ))}
                  </div>
                </div>

              </div>

              <!-- The one difference -->
              <div class="diff-block">
                <div class="diff-block-title">The one difference that explains everything:</div>
                <div class="diff-items">
                  <div class="diff-item diff-item-csr">
                    <div class="diff-item-label">CSR</div>
                    <p class="card-body"><strong style="color:var(--ink);">Angular (DOM API)</strong> creates DOM nodes.<br/>Rendering Engine paints them.<br/>User waits for all of this. 😩</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="diff-item diff-item-ssr">
                    <div class="diff-item-label">SSR</div>
                    <p class="card-body"><strong style="color:var(--ink);">HTML Parser</strong> creates DOM nodes from server HTML.<br/>Rendering Engine paints immediately.<br/>Angular only wires events later. ✅</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>

            </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ════════════ 02 3 MODES ════════════ -->
      <section data-sid="modes" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 · The Solution</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">3 Ways Angular Can <span>Deliver a Page</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          The root problem: the browser does all the work, every time, even when nothing changes between users.
          Angular offers three strategies for where and when the page gets built.
        </p>

        <div class="g3">
          <div class="mode-card csr">
            <div class="mode-emoji">🧑‍🍳</div>
            <span class="chip chip-red">CSR — Client</span>
            <p class="mode-title">The DIY Kitchen</p>
            <p class="mode-desc">
              Restaurant ships you <strong>raw ingredients + recipe</strong>.
              You cook at home. Food only arrives after you finish.
            </p>
            <div class="mode-analogy">
              JS bundle = ingredients<br/>
              Angular = recipe<br/>
              Browser = your kitchen
            </div>
            <div class="mode-pros">
              <span class="pro">✓ Great for auth-only apps</span>
              <span class="pro">✓ No server at runtime</span>
              <span class="con">✗ Slow first load (2–5s)</span>
              <span class="con">✗ Poor SEO</span>
            </div>
            <div class="mode-code">RenderMode.Client</div>
          </div>

          <div class="mode-card ssr">
            <div class="mode-emoji">🍽️</div>
            <span class="chip chip-blue">SSR — Server</span>
            <p class="mode-title">Made to Order</p>
            <p class="mode-desc">
              You sit at a restaurant. The <strong>chef cooks it fresh</strong>
              and serves a ready plate — no cooking required by you.
            </p>
            <div class="mode-analogy">
              Server = chef running Angular<br/>
              Cooking = server builds HTML<br/>
              Ready plate = full HTML sent
            </div>
            <div class="mode-pros">
              <span class="pro">✓ Fast first paint (&lt;1s)</span>
              <span class="pro">✓ Great SEO</span>
              <span class="pro">✓ Personalised per user</span>
              <span class="con">✗ Server needed per request</span>
            </div>
            <div class="mode-code">RenderMode.Server</div>
          </div>

          <div class="mode-card ssg">
            <div class="mode-emoji">🥡</div>
            <span class="chip chip-green">SSG — Prerender</span>
            <p class="mode-title">The Meal-Prep Box</p>
            <p class="mode-desc">
              Cook all meals on Sunday, pack in boxes.
              When hungry — open and eat instantly.
              <strong>Same meal for every visitor.</strong>
            </p>
            <div class="mode-analogy">
              Build time = cook all pages<br/>
              Box = pre-built HTML files<br/>
              CDN = instant delivery
            </div>
            <div class="mode-pros">
              <span class="pro">✓ Instant load, no server</span>
              <span class="pro">✓ Best for blogs, docs</span>
              <span class="con">✗ Same for all users</span>
              <span class="con">✗ Stale if data changes</span>
            </div>
            <div class="mode-code">RenderMode.Prerender</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ════════════ 03 MATRIX ════════════ -->
      <section data-sid="matrix" class="section sec-panel">

          <!-- Text -->
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">03 · Comparison</p>
            <h2 class="sec-title">The <span>Rendering Matrix</span></h2>
            <p class="sec-lead">
              Same Angular app, three different delivery strategies.
              Pick the mode based on what each route actually needs —
              not one mode for everything.
            </p>
            <div class="callout callout-green">
              <header class="callout-title callout-title-green">💡 SSG = Prerender — same thing</header>
              The web community calls it <strong>SSG</strong>.
              Angular's API calls it <strong>RenderMode.Prerender</strong>.
              Both mean HTML built at deploy time, served as a static file from CDN.
