      <!-- ════════════ 04 MENTAL MODELS ════════════ -->
      <section data-sid="mental-models" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">04 · Mental Models</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Three Analogies That Make <span>SSR Click</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          Abstract concepts are hard to hold. These three mental models anchor every SSR idea
          to something concrete — so it sticks the first time.
        </p>

        <!-- Three Worlds -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">The Three Worlds Angular Operates In</header>
          <div class="world-row mb-md">
            <div class="world-card">
              <div class="world-icon">🖥️</div>
              <p class="world-name">Node.js Server</p>
              <p class="world-desc">Runs component code. Builds an HTML string. No <code>window</code>, no <code>document</code>, no browser APIs.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="world-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="world-card">
              <div class="world-icon">📡</div>
              <p class="world-name">Network (HTTP)</p>
              <p class="world-desc">Carries only plain text — the HTML string. No JS objects, no component state can travel here.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="world-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="world-card">
              <div class="world-icon">🌐</div>
              <p class="world-name">Browser Tab</p>
              <p class="world-desc">Receives HTML, renders it, downloads Angular JS, then wires Angular logic to the DOM.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="callout callout-blue">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-blue">The constraint that drives everything</header>
            <p>
              <strong style="color:var(--ink);">HTML strings travel. Component objects never do.</strong>
              The server builds a component object, evaluates its template into an HTML string, and sends only that string.
              The component object is destroyed. The browser must build a fresh one from scratch.
              That single fact is why hydration, server-safe code, and the HTTP Transfer Cache all exist.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Restaurant analogy -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">The Restaurant Analogy — Rendering Modes</header>
          <div class="analogy-grid3">
            <div class="sticker sticker-green">
              <div class="sticker-emoji">🍽️</div>
              <header class="sticker-title">Buffet → SSG / Prerender</header>
              <p class="card-body">Food prepared once in the morning. Same plate for every guest. Instant. No chef needed per person.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-blue">
              <div class="sticker-emoji">🛎️</div>
              <header class="sticker-title">Room Service → SSR</header>
              <p class="card-body">Cooked fresh per order. Delivered hot to your door. Takes a few minutes — worth it for a personalised meal.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-red">
              <div class="sticker-emoji">🧃</div>
              <header class="sticker-title">Minibar → CSR</header>
              <p class="card-body">You open it yourself. No staff involved. You know what you want — but you have to wait to unpack it first.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Furnished apartment analogy -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">The Furnished Apartment Analogy — Hydration</header>
          <div class="analogy-grid2">
            <div class="sticker sticker-red">
              <header class="sticker-title">✗ Without Hydration — Demolish &amp; Rebuild</header>
              <p class="card-body">
                The server delivers a <strong style="color:var(--ink);">fully furnished apartment</strong> — all furniture, walls, and fittings are there.
                But Angular (without hydration) walks in, demolishes everything, and rebuilds the exact same apartment from scratch.
                User sees the furniture disappear and reappear. Wasteful and jarring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">✓ With Hydration — Move In &amp; Wire the Electricity</header>
              <p class="card-body">
                Angular walks into the already-furnished apartment and simply <strong style="color:var(--ink);">installs the electricity and plumbing</strong>.
                Furniture stays exactly where it is. No visible disruption. The apartment goes from static to interactive
                without the user noticing anything change.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Chef analogy for Transfer Cache -->
        <div>
          <header class="subsec-label">The Chef Analogy — HTTP Transfer Cache</header>
          <div class="callout callout-green">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-green">👨‍🍳 The chef can't travel — but the recipe can</header>
            <p class="card-body">
              The server is a chef who prepares a dish and sends it to your table.
              You receive the finished dish — the <strong style="color:var(--ink);">HTML string</strong>.
              You can also receive the ingredient list — the <strong style="color:var(--ink);">JSON data</strong> — alongside it.
            </p>
            <p class="card-body">
              But you <em>cannot</em> receive the chef. The chef's knowledge of when the butter is about to burn.
              The chef's hands. Those are <strong style="color:var(--ink);">live capabilities that only exist in the chef's body</strong>.
              The browser must grow its own chef (component objects) from scratch —
              which is exactly why Angular boots fresh and why the HTTP Transfer Cache passes the ingredient list
              (JSON data) in the HTML, so the browser's chef doesn't have to re-fetch the same ingredients.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </section>

