      <!-- ════════════ 07 SERVER ROUTING ════════════ -->
      <section data-sid="server-routing" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">07 · Server Routing</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">The Problem with Treating Every Route the <span>Same</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          When Angular SSR is on, the server runs Angular and builds HTML for every request —
          no matter which page is being asked for. That sounds fine until you realise
          your app has pages that are fundamentally different from each other.
        </p>

        <!-- Restaurant Analogy -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">The Problem — A Restaurant Analogy</header>

          <div class="sticker sticker-top-orange mb-md">
            <header class="sticker-title">🍽️ Imagine your restaurant has 3 items on the menu</header>
            <div class="menu-items">
              <div class="menu-item">
                <span class="menu-icon">💧</span>
                <p class="card-body"><strong style="color:var(--ink);">Packaged water</strong> — same for every customer, never changes. You can stock 1000 bottles in the morning and hand them out all day. There is zero reason to "prepare" a new bottle for each customer.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="menu-item">
                <span class="menu-icon">🍲</span>
                <p class="card-body"><strong style="color:var(--ink);">Today's special</strong> — same for all customers today, but changes daily. Cook a big batch in the morning, keep it warm, serve all day. Throw away at night.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="menu-item">
                <span class="menu-icon">🎂</span>
                <p class="card-body"><strong style="color:var(--ink);">Custom birthday cake</strong> — completely unique per customer, made fresh only when ordered. You cannot prepare this in advance. It must be made on-demand.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="callout callout-red-left mb-md">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-red">Now imagine your kitchen has ONE rule for everything:</header>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--ink);">"Cook everything fresh, to order, for every customer, every time."</strong><br/>The water? You open a new bottle for every person — wasted effort. The special? You cook it fresh each time — also wasted. The cake? Correct — but only by accident. <strong style="color:var(--red);">One kitchen rule applied to everything = two out of three are wrong.</strong></p>
          </div>

          <div class="callout callout-green">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-green">This is exactly what Angular SSR does by default</header>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--ink);">/home</strong> = packaged water. Same HTML for every user. But SSR rebuilds it fresh on every request anyway — wasted.<br/><strong style="color:var(--ink);">/products/123</strong> = today's special. Changes daily but same for all users. Could be cached, but SSR rebuilds it every time.<br/><strong style="color:var(--ink);">/dashboard</strong> = birthday cake. Unique per user. Must be fresh. SSR handles this correctly — but only by accident.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- The Solution -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">The Solution — Give Each Route Its Own Rule</header>
          <div class="sol-steps">
            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-red">1</div>
              <div>
                <p class="card-title">Before SSR — the server was completely blind to routes</p>
                <p class="card-body">In a normal Angular app there is no real server. Every URL — <code>/home</code>, <code>/dashboard</code>, <code>/products/123</code> — returns the exact same empty <code>index.html</code> + JS bundle. Angular boots in the browser and figures out which page to show. The server has no idea what route was requested.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-orange">2</div>
              <div>
                <p class="card-title">With SSR — the server finally understands which route is being requested</p>
                <p class="card-body">Now Angular runs on the server. The server knows whether you hit <code>/home</code> or <code>/dashboard</code>. It runs the right component, fetches the right data, and sends back a fully-built HTML page. But by default, it does this the same way for every route — fresh render, every time, no exceptions.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-green">3</div>
              <div>
                <p class="card-title">Server Routing — a new file where you write per-route instructions</p>
                <p class="card-body">Angular 17+ introduced <code>app.routes.server.ts</code> — a separate file that sits alongside your existing <code>app.routes.ts</code>. Your existing routes are untouched. This file only adds server-side behaviour on top: <em>"for /home, build it once at deploy."</em> <em>"for /dashboard, render fresh every time, never cache."</em> <em>"for /old-page, send a 301 redirect."</em></p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 3 Route Types -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">Your App's Routes Fall Into 3 Types</header>
          <div class="g3">
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-green">
              <div class="sticker-emoji">💧</div>
              <header class="sticker-title">/home, /about</header>
              <p class="worker-action">Prerender — build once</p>
              <p class="card-body">Never changes. Same HTML for every user. Build at deploy time, serve as a static file forever. Zero server cost per request.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-blue">
              <div class="sticker-emoji">🛍️</div>
              <header class="sticker-title">/products/:id</header>
              <p class="worker-action">Server + Cache</p>
              <p class="card-body">Changes when stock or price changes — same for all users. Server-render once, cache for an hour, serve many users from that cache.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-red">
              <div class="sticker-emoji">👤</div>
              <header class="sticker-title">/dashboard</header>
              <p class="worker-action">Server — never cache</p>
              <p class="card-body">Unique per user, changes in real time. Must render fresh on the server for every single request. Caching would show User A's data to User B.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Prerender vs Server+Cache -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">Prerender vs Server + Cache — What Actually Happens</header>
          <div class="g2 mb-md">
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">💧 Prerender — /home, /about</header>
              <div class="explain-rows">
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">①</span><span>At <strong style="color:var(--ink);">deploy time</strong>, Angular builds the HTML once for this page</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">②</span><span>That HTML is saved as a <strong style="color:var(--ink);">static file</strong> on the server</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">③</span><span>User 1, User 50, User 100 — all get <strong style="color:var(--ink);">that exact same file</strong>. Server never runs Angular again</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-green">④</span><span>Page only changes when you <strong style="color:var(--ink);">deploy again</strong></span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sticker sticker-left-blue">
              <header class="sticker-title">🛍️ Server + Cache — /products/:id</header>
              <div class="explain-rows">
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-blue">①</span><span><strong style="color:var(--ink);">User 1</strong> requests the page → server runs Angular, fetches data, builds HTML, sends it</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-blue">②</span><span>Server tells the browser/CDN: <strong style="color:var(--ink);">"keep this for 1 hour"</strong> (via <code>Cache-Control</code>)</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-blue">③</span><span><strong style="color:var(--ink);">Users 2–100</strong> within that hour → get the cached copy. Server is never touched</span></div>
                <div class="explain-row"><span class="explain-num explain-num-blue">④</span><span>After 1 hour, cache expires → <strong style="color:var(--ink);">next request triggers a fresh build</strong> and the cycle repeats</span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="callout callout-blue">
            <header class="callout-title callout-title-blue">The key difference</header>
            <p><strong style="color:var(--ink);">Prerender</strong> is built at deploy time — never rebuilds unless you redeploy. Perfect for pages that never change.<br/><strong style="color:var(--ink);">Server + Cache</strong> is built on the first request after the cache expires — perfect for pages that change (stock, price) but are the same for all users within that window.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- The File -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">The File: <code style="font-size:.7rem;text-transform:none;">app.routes.server.ts</code></header>
          <div class="code-wrap">
            <div class="code-bar">
              <div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div>
              <span class="code-filename">app.routes.server.ts</span>
            </div>
            <div class="code-body">
              <span class="ky">import</span> &#123; RenderMode, ServerRoute &#125; <span class="ky">from</span> <span class="st">'@angular/ssr'</span>;<br/><br/>
              <span class="ky">export const</span> <span class="fn">serverRoutes</span>: <span class="fn">ServerRoute</span>[] = [<br/><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// 💧 Packaged water — build once at deploy, serve forever</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&#123; <span class="hi">path</span>: <span class="st">''</span>, <span class="hi">renderMode</span>: RenderMode.<span class="ok">Prerender</span> &#125;,<br/><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// 🍲 Today's special — render fresh, cache for 1 hour</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&#123;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hi">path</span>: <span class="st">'products/:id'</span>,<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hi">renderMode</span>: RenderMode.<span class="ok">Server</span>,<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hi">headers</span>: &#123; <span class="st">'Cache-Control'</span>: <span class="st">'max-age=3600'</span> &#125;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&#125;,<br/><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// 🎂 Birthday cake — render fresh every time, never cache</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&#123;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hi">path</span>: <span class="st">'dashboard'</span>,<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hi">renderMode</span>: RenderMode.<span class="ok">Server</span>,<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hi">headers</span>: &#123; <span class="st">'Cache-Control'</span>: <span class="st">'no-store'</span> &#125;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&#125;,<br/><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Old URL — send browser + search engines to the new one</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&#123; <span class="hi">path</span>: <span class="st">'old-products'</span>, <span class="hi">redirectTo</span>: <span class="st">'/products'</span>, <span class="hi">status</span>: <span class="vl">301</span> &#125;,<br/><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Catch-all — anything unknown gets a real 404</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&#123; <span class="hi">path</span>: <span class="st">'**'</span>, <span class="hi">renderMode</span>: RenderMode.<span class="ok">Server</span>, <span class="hi">status</span>: <span class="vl">404</span> &#125;<br/>
              ];
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Two Files Side by Side -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">How <code style="font-size:.7rem;text-transform:none;">app.routes.ts</code> and <code style="font-size:.7rem;text-transform:none;">app.routes.server.ts</code> Work Together</header>
          <p class="card-body">You do not change your existing <code>app.routes.ts</code> at all. These two files answer <em>different questions</em> about the same URL.</p>
