              <div class="code-body" style="border-radius:0 0 var(--radius) var(--radius);font-size:.7rem;line-height:1.9;border-top:var(--border-w) solid var(--border);">
                <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Same DOM Node — not recreated, no flicker:</span><br/>
                <span class="fn">document</span>.<span class="hi">querySelector</span>(<span class="st">'h1'</span>).<span class="hi">textContent</span>&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// "Hello, World!"  ✅ still visible</span><br/>
                <span class="fn">document</span>.<span class="hi">querySelector</span>(<span class="st">'h1'</span>).<span class="hi">onclick</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// [handler]        ✅ Angular wired it</span><br/>
                <span class="fn">document</span>.<span class="hi">querySelector</span>(<span class="st">'h1'</span>).<span class="hi">__ngContext__</span>&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// HelloComponent   ✅ component attached</span><br/>
                <br/>
                <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Mutate the property → DOM updates instantly:</span><br/>
                <span class="hi">comp</span>.name = <span class="st">'Angular'</span>;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// → &lt;h1&gt; updates: "Hello, Angular!" ✅</span>
              </div>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Key insight -->
        <div class="callout callout-green">
          <header class="callout-title callout-title-green">🔑 Component Object and DOM Node are always two separate things</header>
          <p>
            Angular always maintains two separate things: a <strong style="color:var(--ink);">component object</strong> in JavaScript memory
            and a <strong style="color:var(--ink);">DOM node</strong> in the browser's tree.
            <strong style="color:var(--ink);">Hydration is the act of connecting those two things</strong> —
            so that when the component object's property changes, the DOM node updates.
            The HTML string travels. The Component Object never does.
            That is the core constraint that makes all of SSR necessary.
          </p>
        </div>

      </section>

      <!-- ════════════ 08 HTTP CACHE ════════════ -->
      <section data-sid="http-cache" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">08 · HTTP Transfer Cache</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Why Your API Gets Called <span>Twice</span> — And How to Fix It</h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          When Angular boots in the browser, it creates fresh component objects with no memory of what ran on the server.
          That means <code>ngOnInit</code> fires again — and with it, your <code>http.get()</code> calls.
          The data is already in the DOM, but Angular fetches it again anyway.
          Transfer Cache stops this.
        </p>

        <!-- Before / After -->
        <div class="g2 mb-lg">
          <div class="sticker sticker-red">
            <header class="sticker-title">❌ Without Transfer Cache</header>
            <div class="sticker-list">
              <p>🖥️ Server: <code>ngOnInit</code> → GET /api/products → data arrives</p>
              <p>⚙️ Builds HTML with products in it</p>
              <p>📤 HTML sent to browser</p>
              <p>🌐 Browser renders DOM nodes — user sees the list</p>
              <p>⬇️ Angular JS downloads, boots, creates fresh component</p>
              <p>🔁 ngOnInit fires AGAIN in browser</p>
              <p>📡 GET /api/products — same data, again</p>
              <p>⏳ UI flickers / spinner for data already in DOM</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="sticker sticker-green">
            <header class="sticker-title">✅ With Transfer Cache</header>
            <div class="sticker-list">
              <p>🖥️ Server: <code>ngOnInit</code> → GET /api/products → data arrives</p>
              <p>⚙️ Builds HTML + <strong style="color:var(--ink);">embeds JSON in &lt;script&gt; tag</strong></p>
              <p>📤 HTML + embedded JSON sent together (one HTTP response)</p>
              <p>🌐 Browser renders DOM nodes — user sees the list</p>
              <p>⬇️ Angular JS downloads, boots, creates fresh component</p>
              <p class="callout-point">🔁 ngOnInit fires — http.get() is called</p>
              <p class="callout-point">📦 Transfer Cache intercepts: returns from &lt;script&gt;</p>
              <p class="callout-point">⚡ Zero network request. Zero flicker.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- How it works -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">How It Works — Step by Step</header>
          <div class="tc-steps">
            {[
              { n:'1', label:'Server: ngOnInit runs — Transfer Cache watches silently', body:'Your component calls http.get(\'/api/products\') on the server. Transfer Cache intercepts it and quietly copies the API response. Your component is unaffected.' },
              { n:'2', label:'Server: JSON embedded in HTML as a <script> tag', body:'Transfer Cache writes the API response as plain JSON text into a <script type="application/json" id="ng-transfer-state"> tag, right inside the HTML.' },
              { n:'3', label:'One HTTP response carries both: HTML + data', body:'Browser receives a single response — rendered HTML and embedded JSON travel together. No extra round-trip. Data is there before Angular even boots.' },
              { n:'4', label:'Browser: ngOnInit fires — intercepted instantly, zero network', body:'Angular boots, calls http.get() — Transfer Cache intercepts it, reads the JSON from the ng-transfer-state tag, returns it instantly. Zero network request, zero spinner.' },
            ].map(({ n, label, body }) => (
              <div class="tc-step">
                <div class="tc-num">{n}</div>
                <div>
                  <header class="sticker-title">{label}</header>
                  <p class="card-body">{body}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Config code -->
        <div class="mb-md">
          <header class="subsec-label">Enable It — One Line in app.config.ts</header>
          <div class="code-wrap">
            <div class="code-bar">
              <div class="code-dots">
                <div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div>
                <div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div>
                <div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div>
              </div>
              <span class="code-filename">app.config.ts</span>
            </div>
            <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.72rem;line-height:2;">
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Angular 17</span><br/>
              <span class="fn">provideHttpClient</span>(<span class="fn">withHttpTransferCache</span>())<br/>
              <br/>
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// Angular 18+ (recommended — withFetch() auto-enables Transfer Cache)</span><br/>
              <span class="fn">provideHttpClient</span>(<span class="fn">withFetch</span>())
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout callout-yellow">
          <header class="callout-title callout-title-yellow">⚠️ Never cache authenticated requests</header>
          <p>
            Transfer Cache only runs for GET requests by default. Never enable it for API calls that include
            auth headers — that would cache one user's data and serve it to the next user who hits the same URL.
            Use <code>includeRequestsWithAuthHeaders: false</code> (the default) or skip Transfer Cache
            entirely for auth-gated endpoints.
          </p>
        </div>

      </section>

      <!-- ════════════ 09 SERVER-SAFE ════════════ -->
      <section data-sid="server-safe" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">09 · Server-Safe Code</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Code That Runs in <span>Both Worlds</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          When SSR is enabled, your component runs in two places — the browser <em>and</em> a Node.js server.
          <code>window</code>, <code>document</code>, and <code>localStorage</code> are browser inventions.
          Node.js has never heard of them — calling them server-side crashes the entire request.
        </p>

        <div class="callout callout-yellow mb-lg">
          <header class="callout-title callout-title-yellow">⚠️ The Golden Rule</header>
          Any code touching <code>window</code>, <code>document</code>, or <code>localStorage</code>
          must be wrapped in an <code>isPlatformBrowser()</code> guard.
          HTTP client calls and Angular services are always safe — they work on both sides.
        </div>

        <!-- Part A -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">Part A — Two Environments, Two Completely Different API Surfaces</header>
          <div class="g2">

            <div class="sticker sticker-top-green">
              <header class="sticker-title">🌐 Browser (Chrome / Firefox / Safari)</header>
              <div class="api-rows">
                {[
                  ['window','global scope, scroll, history, location'],
                  ['document','DOM tree, querySelector, title'],
                  ['localStorage','client-side key/value storage'],
                  ['navigator','user agent, geolocation, clipboard'],
                  ['sessionStorage','tab-scoped key/value storage'],
                ].map(([api, desc]) => (
                  <div class="api-row">
                    <code class="api-lbl api-lbl-green">{api}</code>
                    <span class="api-desc">{desc}</span>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
              <p class="card-body">✅ All of these exist in every browser</p>
            </div>

            <div class="sticker sticker-top-red">
              <header class="sticker-title">🖥️ Node.js Server (where SSR runs)</header>
              <div class="api-rows">
                {[
                  ['process','env variables, exit, pid','var(--blue)',false],
                  ['fs / path','file system, path utilities','var(--blue)',false],
                  ['❌ window','undefined — throws ReferenceError','var(--red)',true],
                  ['❌ document','undefined — throws ReferenceError','var(--red)',true],
                  ['❌ localStorage','undefined — throws ReferenceError','var(--red)',true],
                ].map(([api, desc, color, isErr]) => (
                  <div class="api-row">
                    <code style={`color:${color};font-weight:700;white-space:nowrap;`}>{api}</code>
                    <span style={`color:${isErr ? '#dc2626' : 'var(--ink-muted)'};font-weight:${isErr ? '600' : '400'};text-align:right;`}>{desc}</span>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
              <p class="card-body">❌ Node.js has never had browser globals — they simply do not exist</p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Part B -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">Part B — What Actually Happens When Your Component Calls <code style="text-transform:none;letter-spacing:0;font-size:.72rem;">window</code> on the Server</header>
          <div class="sol-steps">
            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-blue">1</div>
              <div>
                <p class="card-title">You enable SSR — Angular now runs your component on the Node.js server</p>
                <p class="card-body">Every time a user hits your URL, Node.js boots an Angular component, calls its lifecycle hooks (including <code>ngOnInit</code>), builds the HTML string, and sends it to the browser. Your component code now runs server-side on every request.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-orange">2</div>
              <div>
                <p class="card-title"><code>ngOnInit</code> fires on the server — and calls <code>window.scrollTo(0, 0)</code></p>
                <p class="card-body">Your component's <code>ngOnInit</code> runs normally. It executes <code>window.scrollTo(0, 0)</code>. Node.js starts looking up <code>window</code> in the JavaScript scope chain — global scope, module scope, function scope...</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sol-step sol-step-err">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-err">3</div>
              <div>
                <header class="sticker-title"><code>window</code> is not in any scope — Node.js throws <code>ReferenceError: window is not defined</code></header>
                <p class="card-body">Node.js can't find <code>window</code> anywhere. JavaScript throws immediately — at the JS engine level, before Angular can react. Angular never gets a chance to catch it or skip it. It's the same as calling any variable that was never declared.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="sol-step sol-step-err">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-err">4</div>
              <div>
                <header class="sticker-title">The entire HTTP request crashes — the user gets a blank page or 500 error</header>
                <p class="card-body">The unhandled error bubbles up through Angular's server renderer, through the Node HTTP server, and kills the request. The browser never receives any HTML. The user sees a blank page or a generic server error — with no indication of what went wrong.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="callout callout-blue-left">
            <p class="card-body">The question you're probably asking: "Why can't Angular just skip browser API calls when running on the server?"</p>
            <p class="card-body">Angular doesn't inspect or intercept your code before running it — it just calls your function. When JavaScript hits <code>window.scrollTo(0, 0)</code>, it looks up <code>window</code> in scope. That lookup fails at the JS engine level, before Angular can do anything. It's not a runtime logic error Angular can catch — it's a missing variable, same as calling <code>foo()</code> when <code>foo</code> was never declared.</p>
            <p class="card-body"><strong style="color:var(--ink);">Think of it like a recipe:</strong> You write a recipe that says "microwave for 2 minutes." That recipe works perfectly at home (browser). When a catering team tries to cook it in a kitchen with no microwave (Node.js server), the entire preparation halts. The fix is to add: "skip this step if there is no microwave." That's exactly what <code>afterNextRender()</code> does.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Part C -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="route-section-title">Part C — Three Ways to Write Server-Safe Code</header>
          <div class="sol-steps">

            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-green">1</div>
              <div style="flex:1;">
                <p class="card-title"><code>afterNextRender()</code> — Angular's "browser only" hook <span style="font-weight:400;color:var(--ink-muted);">(Angular 16+, simplest)</span></p>
                <p class="card-body" style="margin-bottom:.6rem;">Register browser-only code inside this callback. Angular guarantees it will <strong>only ever fire in the browser</strong>, after the first DOM render. The server never calls it. Use this for scroll position, animations, and any third-party library that needs the DOM.</p>
                <div class="code-wrap">
                  <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">component.ts</span></div>
                  <div class="code-body code-body-sm">
                    <span class="ky">constructor</span>() &#123;<br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="fn">afterNextRender</span>(() =&gt; &#123;<br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ok">window</span>.scrollTo(<span class="vl">0</span>, <span class="vl">0</span>);&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ✅ browser only</span><br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ok">localStorage</span>.setItem(<span class="st">'k'</span>, <span class="st">'v'</span>);&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ✅ browser only</span><br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&#125;);<br/>
                    &#125;
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-blue">2</div>
              <div style="flex:1;">
                <p class="card-title"><code>isPlatformBrowser(PLATFORM_ID)</code> — check which environment you're in at runtime</p>
                <p class="card-body" style="margin-bottom:.6rem;">Ask Angular which environment you're in — browser or server. Wrap browser-only code in an <code>if</code> block: the server always gets <code>false</code> and skips it, the browser gets <code>true</code> and runs it. Use this when you need completely different logic on each side.</p>
                <div class="code-wrap">
                  <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">component.ts</span></div>
                  <div class="code-body code-body-sm">
                    <span class="ky">private</span> platformId = <span class="fn">inject</span>(<span class="hi">PLATFORM_ID</span>);<br/>
                    <br/>
                    <span class="fn">ngOnInit</span>() &#123;<br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ky">if</span> (<span class="fn">isPlatformBrowser</span>(<span class="ky">this</span>.platformId)) &#123;<br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ok">window</span>.scrollTo(<span class="vl">0</span>, <span class="vl">0</span>);&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ✅ server skips this entire block</span><br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&#125;<br/>
                    &#125;
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="sol-step">
              <div class="sol-num sol-num-purple">3</div>
              <div style="flex:1;">
                <p class="card-title"><code>inject(DOCUMENT)</code> — Angular's safe document token</p>
                <p class="card-body" style="margin-bottom:.6rem;">Instead of calling <code>document</code> directly (which crashes on the server), ask Angular for it using <code>inject(DOCUMENT)</code>. In the browser Angular gives you the real <code>document</code>. On the server it gives you a safe dummy version that silently does nothing instead of crashing. Use this for <code>document.title</code>, <code>document.cookie</code>, or setting page meta tags that search engines read.</p>
                <div class="code-wrap">
                  <div class="code-bar"><div class="code-dots"><div class="code-dot" style="background:#ff5f57;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#febc2e;"></div><div class="code-dot" style="background:#28c840;"></div></div><span class="code-filename">component.ts</span></div>
                  <div class="code-body code-body-sm">
                    <span class="ky">private</span> doc = <span class="fn">inject</span>(<span class="hi">DOCUMENT</span>);<br/>
                    <br/>
                    <span class="fn">ngOnInit</span>() &#123;<br/>
                    &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ky">this</span>.doc.title = <span class="st">'My Page'</span>;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ✅ safe on server AND browser</span><br/>
                    &#125;
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Before / After -->
        <div class="g2">
          <div class="ba-code-panel ba-code-bad">
            <div class="ba-code-head">
              <header>❌ Crashes on the server</header>
            </div>
            <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.7rem;border-radius:0;">
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ngOnInit runs on SERVER too — no browser!</span><br/>
              <span class="fn">ngOnInit</span>() &#123;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="rd">window</span>.scrollTo(<span class="vl">0</span>, <span class="vl">0</span>);&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// 💥 ReferenceError</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="rd">localStorage</span>.getItem(<span class="st">'x'</span>);&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// 💥 ReferenceError</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="rd">document</span>.title = <span class="st">'X'</span>;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// 💥 ReferenceError</span><br/>
              &#125;
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="ba-code-panel ba-code-good">
            <div class="ba-code-head">
              <header>✅ Safe — runs only in browser</header>
            </div>
            <div class="code-body" style="font-size:.7rem;border-radius:0;">
              <span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// afterNextRender = browser only, guaranteed</span><br/>
              <span class="fn">afterNextRender</span>(() =&gt; &#123;<br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ok">window</span>.scrollTo(<span class="vl">0</span>, <span class="vl">0</span>);&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ✅ browser only</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ok">localStorage</span>.getItem(<span class="st">'x'</span>);&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ✅ browser only</span><br/>
              &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="ok">document</span>.title = <span class="st">'X'</span>;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:var(--ink-light);font-style:italic;">// ✅ browser only</span><br/>
              &#125;);
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </section>

      <!-- ════════════ 10 SETUP ════════════ -->
      <section data-sid="setup" class="section sec-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">10 · Setup</p>
        <h2 class="sec-title">How to Add SSR to an <span>Angular App</span></h2>
        <p class="sec-lead">
          Angular SSR is one command away — but understanding what each generated file does
          stops you being confused the first time something doesn't work.
        </p>

        <!-- Two commands -->
        <div class="mental-section">
          <header class="subsec-label">Two commands — choose one</header>
          <div class="g2">
            <div class="code-wrap">
