          {[
            { label:'CSR = browser does all the work', body:'Angular boots in the browser, downloads JS, builds the DOM. Fast for auth apps. Terrible for SEO and slow networks.' },
            { label:'SSR = server runs Angular first', body:'Node.js executes Angular, produces full HTML, and sends it. User sees content in milliseconds. Crawler sees full content.' },
            { label:'SSG (Prerender) = build once, serve forever', body:'HTML built at deploy time, served from CDN as static files. Fastest possible delivery. Only for content identical for all users.' },
            { label:'The Three Worlds constraint', body:'Server → Network → Browser. HTML strings travel across HTTP. Component objects never do — they are destroyed on the server the moment the HTML is sent.' },
            { label:'Hydration stops DOM recreation', body:'Without hydration Angular throws away the server-rendered DOM and rebuilds it. With hydration it claims existing DOM nodes and only attaches event handlers.' },
            { label:'Hybrid rendering = right tool per route', body:'app.routes.server.ts assigns a rendering strategy per route. /home → Prerender. /profile → Server. /dashboard → Client.' },
            { label:'Component Object and DOM Node are always two separate things', body:'The Component Object lives in JS memory. The DOM Node lives in the browser tree. Hydration is the act of connecting them so property changes update the screen.' },
            { label:'HTTP Transfer Cache stops the double-fetch', body:"ngOnInit fires twice — once on server, once in browser. Transfer Cache embeds the server's API response in the HTML as JSON, so the browser reuses it instead of re-fetching." },
            { label:'Component code runs twice — guard browser APIs', body:'Your component runs once on the server (produces HTML) and once in the browser (wires events). Guard window, document, localStorage with isPlatformBrowser() or afterNextRender().' },
            { label:'app.routes.server.ts answers "how to serve it"', body:'app.routes.ts answers "which component?" — app.routes.server.ts answers "renderMode, headers, status code." Two files, same paths, different questions.' },
            { label:'HTML string travels. Component Object never does.', body:'That single constraint is why SSR, hydration, server-safe code, and the HTTP Transfer Cache all exist. Everything follows from it.' },
